    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "4C+37.11 is a massive luminous elliptical galaxy harboring the closest spatially resolved supermassive black hole (SMBH) binary observed at a projected separation of of 7.3 parsecs. This binary is likely to have formed as a result of a past galactic merger, during which its component black holes scoured the resulting galaxy of stellar and gaseous material while inspiraling to a common center. The close separation of the central SMBH binary makes 4C+37.11 an important case study for both host galaxy-black hole coevolution and the final parsec problem of SMBH binary mergers. We present optical integral field and infrared long-slit spectroscopic observations of the core of 4C+37.11. Jeans Anisotropic Modeling of the stellar kinematics reveal a total binary mass of 28 billion solar masses and corroborate existing host galaxy-black hole correlations. Optical and infrared emission line studies further reveal a dearth of gaseous material in comparison to the total binary mass needed for the black holes to overcome their separation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Surti, T. (2024). The Dynamics and Black Hole Binary Mass of 4C+37.11. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/tz804fc0550. https://doi.org/10.25740/tz804fc0550.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
